<div class="bhd-page__keypoints" aria-label="Glucose and water absorption at a glance">
  <div><strong>Sodium + glucose</strong><span>SGLT1 couples the uptake of sodium and glucose across the brush-border membrane of small-intestinal cells.</span></div>
  <div><strong>A maintained gradient</strong><span>The sodium-potassium ATPase on the opposite side of the cell helps preserve the sodium gradient that makes cotransport possible.</span></div>
  <div><strong>Concentration matters</strong><span>The benefit comes from appropriate formulation, not from adding the maximum possible amount of sugar or salt.</span></div>
</div>

<div class="bhd-page__prose-grid">
  <article class="bhd-page__prose">
    <p class="bhd-page__kicker">The central mechanism</p>
    <h2>SGLT1 links glucose transport to sodium transport</h2>
    <p>The surface of the small intestine is lined with absorptive epithelial cells called enterocytes. On the side facing the intestinal lumen, the sodium-glucose cotransporter 1, or <strong>SGLT1</strong>, uses the inward sodium gradient to move glucose into those cells together with sodium.</p>
    <p>This mechanism became extraordinarily important to medicine because glucose-stimulated sodium absorption remains useful even in many secretory diarrhoeal illnesses. Oral rehydration therapy takes advantage of that preserved transport pathway to help replace water and electrolytes lost through diarrhoea.</p>
  </article>
  <aside class="bhd-page__callout">
    <span class="bhd-page__callout-label">Key point</span>
    <h3>Glucose is useful here because of transport physiology, not because sugar itself is “hydrating.”</h3>
    <p>The relevant effect depends on glucose, sodium, solution concentration and the intestinal transport system working together.</p>
  </aside>
</div>

<section class="bhd-page__cards-section">
  <p class="bhd-page__kicker">Step by step</p>
  <h2>How sodium-glucose cotransport supports net water absorption</h2>
  <div class="bhd-page__steps">
    <article class="bhd-page__step"><h3>Glucose and sodium arrive in the small-intestinal lumen</h3><p>A drink or meal is digested and mixed with gastrointestinal secretions. Glucose and sodium are then available at the brush-border surface of enterocytes.</p></article>
    <article class="bhd-page__step"><h3>SGLT1 cotransports sodium and glucose into the enterocyte</h3><p>SGLT1 binds glucose and sodium on the luminal side and transports them into the cell. The process is driven indirectly by the sodium electrochemical gradient.</p></article>
    <article class="bhd-page__step"><h3>The sodium-potassium pump maintains the gradient</h3><p>On the basolateral membrane, Na+/K+-ATPase uses cellular energy to move sodium out of the enterocyte and potassium into it. This keeps intracellular sodium relatively low and sustains the driving force for SGLT1.</p></article>
    <article class="bhd-page__step"><h3>Glucose leaves the cell toward the circulation</h3><p>Glucose exits across the basolateral membrane through facilitated glucose transport, allowing continued luminal uptake when conditions are favourable.</p></article>
    <article class="bhd-page__step"><h3>Water follows the resulting absorptive conditions</h3><p>Solute transport creates local osmotic forces that favour net water movement from the intestinal lumen toward the body. Research also describes water movement through or closely associated with SGLT1, while the exact microscopic pathways remain an active mechanistic topic.</p></article>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="bhd-page__split">
  <div class="bhd-page__split-media">
    <img src="https://images.pexels.com/photos/1435735/pexels-photo-1435735.jpeg?auto=compress&cs=tinysrgb&w=1400" alt="Fresh citrus fruit and water ingredients on a table" width="1400" height="950" loading="lazy" decoding="async">
