  <div class="bhd-page__split-copy">
    <p class="bhd-page__kicker">Formulation</p>
    <h2>More glucose does not automatically mean more water absorption</h2>
    <p>Transporters have finite capacity, and the total concentration of dissolved particles affects how water moves across the intestinal wall. Very concentrated drinks can increase luminal osmotic load and may slow gastric emptying or reduce net water absorption in some settings.</p>
    <p>This is one reason oral rehydration formulations are designed around <strong>ratios and total osmolarity</strong>, not simply the presence of glucose. WHO’s reduced-osmolarity ORS, for example, contains 75 mmol/L sodium and 75 mmol/L glucose with a total osmolarity of 245 mOsm/L.</p>
    <p>Those therapeutic specifications are specific to WHO ORS. They should not be assumed to describe every electrolyte or sports beverage.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="bhd-page__feature">
  <div>
    <p class="bhd-page__kicker">Why ORS changed medicine</p>
    <h2>A transport mechanism became a practical treatment</h2>
    <p>In acute diarrhoeal disease, intestinal secretion can be markedly increased. Yet sodium-glucose cotransport can remain functional. Combining glucose and electrolytes in an appropriately formulated oral solution therefore provides a route for continued sodium and water absorption even while fluid is being lost.</p>
    <p>WHO and UNICEF recommend reduced-osmolarity oral rehydration salts as a medicine for prevention and treatment of dehydration from diarrhoea in appropriate cases. Severe dehydration, shock, persistent vomiting, inability to drink and other red flags require clinical assessment and may require intravenous treatment.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="bhd-page__cards-section">
  <p class="bhd-page__kicker">What this mechanism does not prove</p>
  <h2>Keep product claims separate from general physiology</h2>
  <div class="bhd-page__cards bhd-page__cards--4">
    <article><h3>Not “more sugar is better”</h3><p>Glucose can support sodium cotransport, but excessive carbohydrate concentration can work against rapid fluid delivery or gastrointestinal comfort.</p></article>
    <article><h3>Not every drink is ORS</h3><p>WHO ORS is a defined therapeutic formulation. A consumer electrolyte drink may use related physiology without being equivalent to that medicine.</p></article>
    <article><h3>Not proof of superiority</h3><p>Knowing the SGLT1 mechanism does not establish that one commercial product hydrates faster or better than another. Comparative product claims require appropriate product-specific evidence.</p></article>
    <article><h3>Not a substitute for care</h3><p>Clinically important dehydration and electrolyte disorders need appropriate assessment. A consumer hydration product should not delay needed medical treatment.</p></article>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="bhd-page__brand-context">
  <div>
    <p class="bhd-page__kicker">Best Hydrate context</p>
    <h2>Why glucose is included in our current formula</h2>
    <p>Best Hydrate includes glucose alongside sodium-containing ingredients and other electrolytes. The formulation choice is informed in part by the well-established physiology of sodium-glucose cotransport and by the practical role carbohydrate can play in a hydration beverage.</p>
    <p>That physiological rationale is distinct from claiming that Best Hydrate is WHO ORS, that it treats diarrhoeal dehydration, or that it has demonstrated superior absorption versus another product. Those stronger claims would require the relevant formulation and product-specific clinical evidence.</p>
